    "result_text": "The build is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe change is implemented and the test suite passes (`BUILD SUCCESS`, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`).\n\n**What I found:** The global `ExceptionControllerAdvice` already builds an RFC7807 `ProblemDetail` (with `type`, `title`, `status`, and `detail`) for every rejection path:\n- **400** \u2014 `InvalidOwnerFieldsException` and `MethodArgumentNotValidException`\n- **409** \u2014 `DuplicateOwnerIdentityException` and `OwnerCityAtCapacityException`\n- **429** \u2014 `DailyOwnerLimitReachedException`\n\n**What I changed** (`ExceptionControllerAdvice.java`): I made the `application/problem+json` content type explicit on every problem response rather than relying on framework content negotiation. Each handler now returns:\n\n```java\nreturn ResponseEntity.status(status).contentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_PROBLEM_JSON).body(detail);\n```\n\nThis guarantees the exact `Content-Type: application/problem+json` header the spec requires for all 400/409/429 rejections (and, for consistency, the 404/500 problem responses too), while leaving the HTTP status codes and body fields unchanged. The new acceptance test `coreRejectionIsProblemJson` \u2014 which asserts a 400 rejection returns `application/problem+json` with `$.status == 400` \u2014 passes.",
